      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by letting the unknown number be \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, we need to find 10% of \\( x \\). This can be expressed as:\n\\[\n10\\% \\text{ of } x = \\frac{10}{100} \\times x = \\frac{1}{10} \\times x = \\frac{x}{10}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find 20% of 10% of \\( x \\). This can be expressed as:\n\\[\n20\\% \\text{ of } 10\\% \\text{ of } x = 20\\% \\times 10\\% \\times x = \\frac{20}{100} \\times \\frac{10}{100} \\times x = \\frac{20}{10000} \\times x = \\frac{2}{1000} \\times x = \\frac{x}{500}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that 20% of 10% of \\( x \\) is 12. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{500} = 12\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we solve for \\( x \\) by multiplying both sides of the equation by 500:\n\\[\nx = 12 \\times 500 = 6000\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find 10% of 20% of \\( x \\). This can be expressed as:\n\\[\n10\\% \\text{ of } 20\\% \\text{ of } x = 10\\% \\times 20\\% \\times x = \\frac{10}{100} \\times \\frac{20}{100} \\times x = \\frac{10}{10000} \\times x = \\frac{2}{1000} \\times x = \\frac{x}{500}\n\\]\n\nSince we already know that \\(\\frac{x}{500} = 12\\), we can substitute this value into the expression:\n\\[\n10\\% \\text{ of } 20\\% \\text{ of } x = 12\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
